WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:05.550
SEAL is a workbench for editing and
analyzing DNA, RNA, and protein

2
00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.120
on your Mac. Start with the built-in Sample
Lab.

3
00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:12.790
These synthetic examples are included in
the app, so you can follow

4
00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.840
along with the same sequences.

5
00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:17.750
In the sidebar, open Synthetic Reporter.

6
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:22.050
A project groups related Entries, and each
Entry holds sequence blocks

7
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.320
and notes. Click Synthetic Reporter DNA.

8
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:29.450
The Inspector now shows its name, length,
and base composition.

9
00:00:30.783 --> 00:00:33.923
For a sequence of your own, choose New
entry and give it

10
00:00:33.953 --> 00:00:37.193
a name. Paste a FASTA record into the
sequence input.

11
00:00:37.223 --> 00:00:40.593
Here, the preview shows FASTA and eighty
base pairs.

12
00:00:40.623 --> 00:00:44.993
Submit it and check the new block’s name,
DNA label, and length.

13
00:00:45.023 --> 00:00:49.033
Drag across some bases to select a range
for analysis or annotation.

14
00:00:49.063 --> 00:00:52.703
To change one base, click to place the
cursor and check substitution

15
00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:56.673
mode. Type a replacement, then press
Command Z to undo.

16
00:00:56.703 --> 00:00:58.283
The original base is restored.

17
00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:04.530
Return to the reporter DNA and open
Translate in the Inspector.

18
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.510
Choose Whole sequence to compare all six
reading frames.

19
00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:12.390
Each frame shows its peptide sequence and
any internal stops.

20
00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:15.730
Click Detail, then Translate to protein.

21
00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.710
Amino-acid letters appear alongside the
DNA, aligned with their codons.

22
00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.790
Open ORFs to inspect candidate coding
regions.

23
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.960
Now select Synthetic Reporter Protein and
open Codons.

24
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:32.550
Back-translation preview shows a proposed
coding sequence marked Draft.

25
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:36.350
Review the target host and codon strategy;
different codon choices can

26
00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.000
encode the same protein.

27
00:01:39.227 --> 00:01:42.077
Open Sequence library to reuse a part.

28
00:01:42.107 --> 00:01:46.517
Choose Vectors, then SEAL Synthetic Cloning
Backbone, and click Add to

29
00:01:46.547 --> 00:01:51.997
workspace. Next, open Cloning and Assembly
and select SEAL Demo Plasmid.

30
00:01:52.027 --> 00:01:56.397
Click Map to inspect its circular sequence
and annotated features.

31
00:01:56.427 --> 00:01:59.717
Selecting a feature highlights the
corresponding region.

32
00:01:59.747 --> 00:02:02.717
Under Actions, open Restriction digest.

33
00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:07.357
Choose Cutters, select AluI, and review the
predicted fragments.

34
00:02:07.387 --> 00:02:10.157
Their lengths add up to the original
plasmid.

35
00:02:10.187 --> 00:02:13.717
Click Digest to add each fragment as a
sequence block.

36
00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:20.060
On the reporter DNA, open Actions and
choose Design primers.

37
00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:24.180
Set the target to bases fifty-one through
five hundred fifty.

38
00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:29.340
Compare the sequences, melting
temperatures, and predicted product length.

39
00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:33.380
Add selected pair saves primer features on
the DNA.

40
00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:38.500
Then open Cloning and Assembly and select
SEAL Golden Gate Sample Assembly

41
00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.400
to inspect its product and linked input
parts.

42
00:02:42.637 --> 00:02:47.767
Open Alignments and choose the synthetic
Sanger example with paired reads.

43
00:02:47.797 --> 00:02:50.827
Fill the window gives the alignment more
space.

44
00:02:50.857 --> 00:02:55.047
Click Next mismatch to move to a difference
between the sequences.

45
00:02:55.077 --> 00:02:59.207
Restore the alignment’s size, then open a
read’s chromatogram.

46
00:02:59.237 --> 00:03:02.917
Compare the called bases with the signal
peaks and quality information

47
00:03:02.947 --> 00:03:05.067
to examine an uncertain position.

48
00:03:06.303 --> 00:03:08.873
In Notebook, create a review note.

49
00:03:08.903 --> 00:03:12.013
Write what you checked and what you’d like
to examine next.

50
00:03:12.043 --> 00:03:14.273
SEAL saves the text as you work.

51
00:03:14.303 --> 00:03:16.313
Return to the reporter DNA.

52
00:03:16.343 --> 00:03:19.433
Press Command K and choose Build report.

53
00:03:19.463 --> 00:03:23.603
In Report handoff, check the source block
and choose your attachments:

54
00:03:23.633 --> 00:03:27.513
FASTA, GenBank, or a styled report, for
example.

55
00:03:27.543 --> 00:03:31.273
Expand Package zip preview to inspect the
file list.

56
00:03:31.303 --> 00:03:36.133
Click Download ZIP. Then open Activity in
the Inspector and find Report

57
00:03:36.163 --> 00:03:41.183
package exported. Check its completed
status and expand the event for details.

58
00:03:42.437 --> 00:03:46.107
To get SEAL, give your preferred coding
agent a SEAL repository you

59
00:03:46.137 --> 00:03:48.337
have access to and ask it to set up the app
on

60
00:03:48.367 --> 00:03:52.437
your Mac. The installation guide covers
source builds and an unnotarized

61
00:03:52.467 --> 00:03:56.717
GitHub DMG when available. A Mac App Store
edition is planned.

62
00:03:56.747 --> 00:04:00.427
You can also connect an agent to your
workspace through the command-line

63
00:04:00.457 --> 00:04:04.297
tools or MCP. Press Command K and choose
Open agent setup for

64
00:04:04.327 --> 00:04:08.147
connection instructions. Choose a written
guide or an individual video

65
00:04:08.177 --> 00:04:10.617
lesson to try a workflow yourself.
